Definition of done:
- Every numbered requirement above is discharged{{ if .OperatorRequirements }}, apart from the operator
  requirements{{ end }}, or your report says which is not and why. Do not decide that for yourself quietly: the review audits against
  this list, and a gap you name is a gap it can act on.
- Smallest diff that does it. Touch no file the requirements do not name or imply.
  If the work genuinely needs a wider blast radius, say so in your report rather
  than widening silently.
- A defect belonging to another item gets filed against that item, not fixed here.
- A defect your own diff introduced is this item's, whichever item names it, and
  so is a pre-existing one standing between you and a numbered requirement.
- Anything shipped must be reachable. Something that exists but is unwired,
  unmounted or unconsumed does not satisfy a requirement.
- Name in your report every behavior this diff changes that no numbered requirement
  names: a mode, a default, an ordering, a lifetime, an error a caller used to
  see. Say why each one is safe. A passing verify command is not that argument — it
  reports the behavior somebody wrote a test for, and a delta nobody declared is
  by definition one nobody tested. Neither is "this project never configures
  that": the operator, the filesystem or the next caller may.
{{- if .VerifyCommand }}
- The verify command passes before commit: {{ .VerifyCommand }}
  Run it in the foreground and wait for it to exit. Never background the verify command
  or anything else: drover reads a process your session started and left
  running as a run that did not finish, and parks it.
{{- end }}
{{- if .Branch }}
- Commit on branch {{ .Branch }} and stop there. Terse commit: subject plus at
  most one short paragraph. **Do not open a pull request and do not push.** drover
  pushes the branch and opens the pull request itself, and only once the merge gate has
  exited 0 and a review has come back clean: one that exists says this branch may
  merge, so one opened by the session that wrote it would say that about work
  nobody has read.
- If {{ .Branch }} already carries commits, this is a correction pass: a review
  found a requirement unmet and the work came back rather than becoming
  somebody's issue. Commit onto that same branch.
{{- else }}
- Work in place; this project keeps no branches. Terse commit if it has a history
  at all: subject plus at most one short paragraph.
{{- end }}
